    {
     "what": "base recipe cited as ref 29 (Pardee AB, Jacob F, Monod J. J Mol Biol 1959;1:165-78), origin of 'medium 63' (M63)",
     "obtained": true,
     "how": "identified from reference list; classic M63 composition confirmed no additional trace-metal or vitamin mix beyond iron and thiamine",
     "added": [
      "confirmed medium identity; no hidden trace/vitamin stock"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "missing_before": [],
   "corrections": [],
   "notes": "M63 is a fully defined minimal medium; the paper's methods spell out the entire composition (NH4/SO4, KH2PO4 phosphate buffer, Fe, Mg, thiamine, carbon source) and classic M63 (ref 29) adds no trace-metal or vitamin mix, so nothing was hidden. Caveat: the paper labels the salts as a '10x' stock yet lists 15 mM (NH4)2SO4 and 100 mM KH2PO4 which already equal standard 1x M63 working concentrations; conc_mM therefore reports these as the working (1x) values (the FeSO4 value, given as 2e-2 mM in 10x, dilutes to ~2e-3 mM = standard M63 1x iron). Ampicillin/kanamycin/chloramphenicol were added only "
  },
